# The web layer: the headless browser a web project's checkup needs, on top of the base every box
# shares. Kept under the image name every project used before kinds existed (`vibeboard-agent:latest`)
# so a project with no recorded kind behaves exactly as it always did. decision 75.
FROM vibeboard-agent:base

# THE SYSTEM LIBRARIES A HEADLESS BROWSER NEEDS, and the reason is the feature checkup rather than
# testing. `usableSection` in src/server/runs/prompt/sections.ts tells a checkup "if it is a page, serve
# it and open it" — and without these, it cannot. Playwright downloads its Chromium happily and the
# binary then refuses to start with `libglib-2.0.so.0: cannot open shared object file`, so for every
# browser-tested project the checkup falls back to reading source. That fallback is not neutral: asked
# whether a calculator worked, a checkup read the file and reported a deleted line as present. Found
# 2026-09-03 by planting that defect on purpose.
#
# THE BROWSER ITSELF SHIPS TOO, not only the libraries. `install-deps` lays down the apt packages and
# stops there, which left a box that still could not open a page: the first checkup to try it would have
# had to download 115 MB before it could answer anything, once per box and again after every rebuild.
# Paying that at build time makes it once per image.
#
# `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS WORKS. The default is `$HOME/.cache/ms-playwright`,
# and at this point in the build HOME is root's — while an agent turn runs as the HOST user's uid, which
# is not known until `docker run`. Browsers under /root would be unreadable by the one process that needs
# them. /opt with `a+rX` is readable by every uid, and the ENV persists into the container so Playwright
# looks there rather than in a home directory it cannot write.
#
# Installed globally at a pinned version and then removed, rather than run through `npx`: a bare name
# through a runner is how you end up executing a package that squatted it. The apt packages and the
# browsers both survive the uninstall, which is the entire point.
ENV PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=/opt/ms-playwright
ARG PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION=1.62.1
RUN npm install -g playwright@${PLAYWRIGHT_VERSION} \
 && playwright install-deps chromium \
 && playwright install chromium \
 && npm uninstall -g playwright \
 && chmod -R a+rX /opt/ms-playwright \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# `install-deps` runs apt, and a dependency away is how `sudo` comes back silently — so the web layer
# re-asserts what the base already proved.
RUN ! command -v sudo >/dev/null 2>&1
